Practical philosophy notes: toward a trial text for critical readers
    : re `critical readers` sc. `senior students` @ 01.lynchpin/00.lynchpin.brec

    break point, tasks to the moral break point:
        + premise: prima facie no known, possible explanans can explain either the safehold
          or the coincidence of (6) (or anything else)
            ∵ all known, possible explanantia for the safehold
              are either null or prima facie indeterminate
    \ however
        + premise: it is clear how {the DE disjunction} might be determined such that it explains
          both the safehold and the coincidence of (6)
            : ad `\{the DE disjunction\}` : the disjunction of the two known design explanantia
              for the safehold, which is to say ‘either (or both) of the two’
            ∵ of the two, the mundane might be so determined by the default ground now known to us,
              and the supernatural by a stable ground of maintenance unknown (at present) to us,
              but known to God
                ∵ the mind of a creative designer (as figures in the two design explanantia) can both
                  conform to practical-rational ground direction and (within range of its competence)
                  cause things (artifacts) as effects of that conformance
                ∵ such|those artifacts would (in the case of a design explanans) include artifacts
                  of cosmic structure such as the safehold
                    ∵ ex hypothesi a competent cosmic designer/creator is given
    \ and yet
        + premise: it is unclear how any of the possible non-null, non-design explanantia for the
          safehold might be determined such that it explains also the coincidence of (6)
            ∵ it is unclear how any might be determined to include a different mechanism or process
              (aside from mind as it figures in the design explanantia) that can do the same work
    \ therefore
        + conclude:
            | {the DE disjunction} {is true}|holds
                ∵ belief in {the DE disjunction} is|{would be} justified
                    ∵ one has reason to believe that {the DE disjunction} {is true}|holds
        + conclude likewise for a moral source
            : ad `likewise` : like the {the DE disjunction} in being true or whatnot
        + enter the possibility of moral law
        + raise the moral-breaking problem
            • no moral law (from the design explanantia) is inferable
                ∵ no principle (by a design explanans) is determined
                    ∵ the motive reason in both design explanantia is indeterminate
                    ∵ no command/principle to employ the safehold is inferable
                        ∵ it may have been given merely as a means, subject to prudence
                            / see e.g. how it figures as a means in the coincidence of (6)
                ∵ (case of mundane design) no principle qua absolute is inferable
                    ∵ possibly ∃ a (cosmos or other) context wherein the principle is not obligatory
                        ∵ the scope of obligation/command is not universal
                            : see `^*admit.+a qualification of scope.+re the case of mundane creators` @
                              02.wrong_rhetoric/._/text.notes.brec
                            ∵ the scope of creation/design is not universal
    conclusion, tasks to the moral conclusion:
        + enter rebasing (wholly or partly) on a truth of reason/logic
            - as a recourse in response to the moral-breaking problem
                : re `moral-breaking problem` see `^*\+ raise the ${same}$`
            - either to backstop the command-based explication|interpretation of moral law against
              its shortcomings, or replace it entirely with logic-based explication|interpretation
            : cf. `^*employ\+ this \*truth of.+reason/logic\* grounding of moral law` @
              02.wrong_rhetoric/._/text.notes.brec
        + argue for (as a truth of logic) a stable ground of maintenance re practical logic
            ∵ for the operation of a stable ground, only maintenance would make sense
                ∵ a finalizable operation would be absurd
                    / elsewhere I’ve quoted ‘break out the booze and have a ball’ in parody of it
            ∵ as the object of that maintenance, the exercise of practical logic is indicated
                ∵ natural selection maintains it
                    ∵ natural selection inherently maintains itself
                    ∵ natural selection inherently is an (instance of the) exercise of practical logic
                        : see `^*\? maybe.+just.+that practical logic inheres in it\?$` @
                          02.wrong_rhetoric/._/text.notes.brec
                ∵ pursuit of the default ground would indicate by example and demonstration both:
                        • the maintenance of such an exercise (of practical logic) via reasoning
                            ∵ pursuit of the default ground would be, in itself,
                              an (instance of the) exercise (via reasoning) of practical logic
                        • the underpinning of that maintenance by a truth
                          of (or at least involving) practical logic
                            / viz. that on which (inference of) the default ground rests
                    : see `not.+so much a negative conclusion.+as.+a positive demonstration`s @
                      ../default/._/text.notes.brec
        + infer by analysis of (the concept of) moral law its conditions
        for( each condition of moral law )
            + argue for it being met
            ?+ what formula for the principle?
                | (in a context of reason) one of (maintaining) a rational will
                    : ad `rational will` : one either directed to a ground
                      or (if and where possible) undirected
                    / cf. Kant’s ‘good will’
                    = the most basic of principles that are proper to reason (the power of mind)
                        : ad `proper to reason` : though not to logic, owing to the involvement of will
        + conclude that a moral law is in force|effect
